"""Original megaloblastic and nonmegaloblastic anemia questions."""
from qbank_authoring import question,ref,finalize
B12=ref('NICE NG239: Vitamin B12 Deficiency in Over 16s','https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng239/chapter/Recommendations')
FO=ref('British Columbia: Cobalamin and Folate Deficiency Guideline','https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/health/practitioner-professional-resources/bc-guidelines/vitamin-b12')
KD=ref('KDIGO: Anemia in CKD Clinical Practice Guideline, 2026','https://kdigo.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/KDIGO-2026-Anemia-in-CKD-Guideline.pdf')
rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))
q('A smear shows macro-ovalocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils in a patient with anemia. Which process is most strongly suggested?', 'Megaloblastic erythropoiesis from impaired DNA synthesis',[
 ('Isolated acute blood loss with an otherwise normal marrow','That does not typically produce this nuclear-maturation pattern.'),('Pure iron deficiency','It generally produces a microcytic pattern rather than macro-ovalocytes with hypersegmentation.'),('Hereditary spherocytosis','The characteristic red cells are spherocytes, not this pattern.')],
 'Nuclear maturation helps distinguish a megaloblastic process', '**B12 and folate deficiency are important causes of impaired DNA synthesis.**',['Selected drugs can produce a similar pattern.','Cytoplasmic maturation can outpace nuclear development.'], '**Establish the cause before choosing replacement alone.**',['Review nutrition, medicines and malabsorption.','Neurological symptoms raise urgency for B12 assessment and treatment.'],[['Smear','Macro-ovalocytes and hypersegmentation'],['Mechanism','Impaired DNA synthesis'],['Causes','B12, folate or selected drug effects']],[B12,FO])
q('Which biochemical pattern most supports B12 deficiency over isolated folate deficiency, after considering renal function?', 'Elevated methylmalonic acid and elevated homocysteine',[
 ('Elevated homocysteine with normal MMA as a specific B12 signature','This can occur with folate deficiency and other causes.'),('Normal MMA with low ferritin as proof of B12 deficiency','This primarily raises an iron question rather than establishing B12 deficiency.'),('High serum iron alone','It does not specifically identify B12 deficiency.')],
 'B12 participates in two clinically useful metabolic pathways', '**B12 deficiency can raise both MMA and homocysteine.**',['Folate deficiency commonly raises homocysteine without raising MMA through the B12 pathway.','Renal impairment can elevate MMA and complicate interpretation.'], '**Use metabolic testing to resolve the right uncertainty.**',['Borderline total B12 with a compatible syndrome may need MMA.','Do not use a single metabolite without the clinical and renal context.'],[['B12 deficiency','MMA and homocysteine may rise'],['Folate deficiency','Homocysteine may rise, MMA usually not from folate alone'],['Confounder','Renal impairment']],[B12,FO])
q('A patient has progressive sensory ataxia and impaired vibration sense but normal Hb and MCV. Can clinically important B12 deficiency still be present?', 'Yes; neurological B12 deficiency can occur without anemia or macrocytosis',[
 ('No, macrocytosis is mandatory for neurological involvement','Hematological and neurological findings need not appear together.'),('No, a normal Hb excludes all nutritional neurological disease','It does not.'),('Only folate deficiency causes this classic myelopathy','B12 deficiency is a key cause and folate alone is not an adequate response.')],
 'Do not make a blood-count abnormality a prerequisite for the diagnosis', '**Posterior-column and peripheral-nerve dysfunction can precede anemia.**',['Other neurological causes remain in the differential.','Clinical suspicion should guide appropriate testing.'], '**Avoid treatment delay when the syndrome is compelling.**',['Obtain diagnostic samples before treatment when feasible.','Do not wait for all results in suspected megaloblastic disease with serious neurological signs.'],[['Hb / MCV','May be normal'],['Neurology','Can be clinically important'],['Action','Prompt assessment and indicated B12 replacement']],[B12],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A person with a vegan diet has low B12 but also takes metformin and has prior gastric surgery. What is the best etiological approach?', 'Assess dietary and absorption-related causes rather than assuming diet is the only mechanism',[
 ('A vegan diet excludes any malabsorptive contribution','Multiple causes can coexist.'),('Metformin proves autoimmune gastritis','Medication effect and autoimmunity are distinct mechanisms.'),('Gastric surgery cannot affect B12 absorption','It can alter acid, intrinsic-factor function or anatomy relevant to absorption.')],
 'A recognizable risk factor should not end the causal assessment', '**B12 deficiency may have more than one contributor.**',['Diet, medicines and operations all matter.','The permanence of the cause affects replacement duration.'], '**Match treatment route and follow-up to the mechanism.**',['A reversible dietary cause differs from an irreversible absorption defect.','Review nutritional support and ongoing medication need.'],[['Diet','Insufficient intake'],['Medicines','Possible impaired handling or absorption'],['Surgery','Potential permanent absorption change']],[B12],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Where is the intrinsic-factor–B12 complex principally absorbed?', 'Terminal ileum',[
 ('Stomach','The stomach contributes acid and intrinsic factor but is not the main uptake site of the complex.'),('Proximal jejunum','This is not the principal receptor-mediated site for the IF–B12 complex.'),('Colon','This is not the main physiological absorption site.')],
 'The absorption pathway spans more than one organ', '**Gastric and pancreatic steps precede ileal uptake.**',['Intrinsic factor is produced by gastric parietal cells.','Terminal ileal disease or resection can impair absorption.'], '**Use anatomy to predict replacement needs.**',['Complete terminal ileal resection can create an irreversible cause.','A normal diet does not overcome every anatomical defect.'],[['Stomach','Acid and intrinsic factor'],['Terminal ileum','Complex uptake'],['Clinical implication','Ileal disease or resection can cause deficiency']],[B12])
q('A B12-deficient patient has suspected autoimmune gastritis but a negative anti-intrinsic-factor antibody result. What is the best interpretation?', 'The negative result does not exclude autoimmune gastritis',[
 ('Autoimmune gastritis is definitively ruled out','Sensitivity is insufficient for that conclusion.'),('The negative test proves dietary deficiency','It does not identify the alternative cause.'),('No further etiological assessment is possible','Additional tests and specialist evaluation can help.')],
 'A relatively specific test can still be incompletely sensitive', '**A negative intrinsic-factor antibody does not settle a strong clinical suspicion.**',['Parietal-cell antibodies, gastrin or gastric assessment may contribute.','Interpret assays using the laboratory guidance.'], '**Treat the deficiency while resolving the cause.**',['Replacement should not be withheld during a prolonged etiological work-up.','A confirmed irreversible cause affects the long-term plan.'],[['Positive IF antibody','Supports autoimmune mechanism'],['Negative IF antibody','Does not exclude'],['Further assessment','Other evidence and specialist review']],[B12],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Under NICE NG239, which replacement strategy is recommended for B12 deficiency caused by confirmed autoimmune gastritis?', 'Lifelong intramuscular B12 replacement',[
 ('A one-week course with permanent cessation after Hb recovery','The absorption problem is irreversible.'),('Folate monotherapy','This does not correct B12 deficiency or protect neurological function.'),('Iron chelation as the specific treatment','This is unrelated to the B12 replacement need.')],
 'An irreversible cause requires ongoing replacement', '**NICE recommends lifelong IM replacement for autoimmune-gastritis–related B12 deficiency.**',['Total gastrectomy and complete terminal ileal resection are other important irreversible contexts.','The question specifies the NICE framework.'], '**Explain the reason for continuing despite improvement.**',['Symptoms and blood counts can improve while the absorption defect persists.','Assess new or worsening symptoms rather than assuming all are residual deficiency.'],[['Cause','Autoimmune gastritis'],['Route under NICE','Intramuscular'],['Duration','Lifelong']],[B12])
q('A patient has suspected megaloblastic anemia with new gait ataxia and a spinal-cord sensory syndrome. Blood samples can be drawn immediately. What is the appropriate treatment timing?', 'Draw diagnostic samples promptly and begin indicated B12 replacement without waiting for all results',[
 ('Wait several weeks for every antibody result before treatment','Delay may worsen neurological injury.'),('Give folate alone while deliberately postponing B12','This can improve the count without addressing the neurological risk.'),('Treat only if macrocytosis becomes extreme','MCV severity does not determine whether urgent neurological treatment is needed.')],
 'Prevent avoidable neurological progression', '**NICE advises against delaying B12 replacement in this high-risk presentation.**',['Obtain pretreatment diagnostic samples when feasible.','Continue to investigate competing neurological causes.'], '**Use a route and regimen appropriate to severity and cause.**',['Significant neurological disease often favors a reliable parenteral approach in clinical practice.','Assess response and the need for ongoing replacement.'],[['Before treatment','Prompt samples if feasible'],['Do not wait for','Complete delayed etiological results'],['Goal','Correct deficiency and limit neurological harm']],[B12],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient develops a B12-like myeloneuropathy after heavy recreational nitrous oxide use, but total serum B12 is not clearly low. Which testing strategy is appropriate?', 'MMA or homocysteine to assess functional B12 disturbance',[
 ('Exclude B12 dysfunction solely from total B12','Functional inactivation may not be reflected by a low total concentration.'),('Use serum ferritin as the specific functional B12 assay','Ferritin assesses a different pathway.'),('Measure only fasting glucose and stop the assessment','That does not address the exposure mechanism.')],
 'Nitrous oxide can inactivate cobalamin function', '**A normal-looking total B12 does not exclude this toxic functional disorder.**',['NICE recommends MMA or homocysteine as initial testing in the suspected nitrous-oxide setting.','Interpret metabolites with renal and nutritional confounders.'], '**Stop the exposure and replace B12 as clinically indicated.**',['Continuing nitrous oxide can defeat treatment.','Neurological recovery can be incomplete and needs follow-up.'],[['Exposure','Nitrous oxide'],['Mechanism','Functional cobalamin inactivation'],['Tests','MMA or homocysteine']],[B12],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Why is folate alone inappropriate when B12 deficiency remains a plausible cause of megaloblastic anemia?', 'It may improve hematological findings while leaving B12-related neurological disease untreated',[
 ('Folate reliably replaces every B12-dependent reaction','The vitamins are not interchangeable.'),('Folate has no role in any anemia','It is effective for true folate deficiency.'),('B12 assessment is unnecessary once Hb starts rising','A blood-count response does not exclude an untreated B12 problem.')],
 'A hematological response is not proof of neurological safety', '**Exclude or treat B12 deficiency when replacing folate in a compatible syndrome.**',['Both deficiencies can coexist.','The historical concern is masking the blood-count clue while neurological injury persists.'], '**Replace the demonstrated deficiencies and identify their causes.**',['Review diet, malabsorption and medicines.','Follow symptoms as well as CBC recovery.'],[['Folate','Can correct folate-related DNA synthesis'],['B12','Needed for its own metabolic and neurological functions'],['Safety','Avoid folate-only masking of B12 deficiency']],[FO,B12])
q('A patient with poor intake and alcohol use has megaloblastic anemia, low folate, elevated homocysteine and no biochemical evidence of B12 deficiency. Which is the most likely nutrient mechanism?', 'Folate deficiency impairing DNA synthesis',[
 ('Isolated iron depletion as the complete explanation','That does not explain the megaloblastic and folate findings.'),('Excess erythropoietin causing iron overload','This does not fit the stated nutrient profile.'),('Intrinsic-factor antibody production as proof from folate alone','Low folate does not establish autoimmune gastritis.')],
 'Folate supply is vulnerable to inadequate intake and increased demand', '**Deficiency can produce ineffective megaloblastic hematopoiesis.**',['Alcohol can impair intake and folate handling.','Malabsorption and selected drugs are other causes.'], '**Replace folate after addressing B12 safety.**',['Treat the nutritional and alcohol-related contributors.','Monitor the response and look for coexisting deficiencies.'],[['Folate','Low'],['Homocysteine','May rise'],['MMA','Not raised through isolated folate deficiency']],[FO],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which medication mechanism can cause megaloblastic change?', 'Antifolate inhibition of DNA synthesis, as with methotrexate',[
 ('A beta-blocker necessarily depleting intrinsic factor in every patient','This is not the standard mechanism.'),('A loop diuretic directly replacing thymidylate synthase','That is not its pharmacology.'),('An inhaled bronchodilator causing obligatory folate blockade','This is not a typical effect.')],
 'Medication history is part of a macrocytosis assessment', '**Antimetabolites can impair nucleotide synthesis.**',['Toxicity depends on dose, renal function and interactions.','Some drugs cause macrocytosis through other mechanisms.'], '**Assess whether the finding is isolated or part of dangerous toxicity.**',['Mucositis, cytopenias or renal deterioration can signal a serious problem.','Use drug-specific interruption and rescue guidance when indicated.'],[['Mechanism','Antifolate effect'],['Example','Methotrexate'],['Danger signs','Mucositis or broader cytopenias']],[FO])
q('In severe untreated megaloblastic anemia, why can LDH be very high with a relatively low reticulocyte response?', 'Ineffective erythropoiesis with intramedullary destruction of abnormal precursors',[
 ('Every high LDH proves brisk effective peripheral regeneration','The site and effectiveness of cell production differ.'),('A low reticulocyte count excludes all red-cell precursor destruction','Intramedullary loss can coexist with low peripheral output.'),('LDH directly measures stored iron only','LDH is a cell-injury marker, not an iron-store assay.')],
 'Cell destruction does not always produce a robust circulating reticulocytosis', '**Abnormal precursor maturation can lead to death within the marrow.**',['Indirect bilirubin can also rise.','This differs from a fully regenerative peripheral hemolytic process.'], '**Interpret the count, smear and biochemical evidence together.**',['Severe B12 deficiency can mimic a microangiopathic picture.','Do not delay urgent TTP care when the actual probability remains high; resolve the differential rapidly.'],[['LDH','Can be markedly elevated'],['Reticulocytes','Inappropriately low'],['Mechanism','Ineffective marrow output']],[B12,FO])
q('Which follow-up finding is expected after effective B12 replacement in megaloblastic anemia?', 'An early reticulocyte response followed by progressive blood-count recovery',[
 ('Permanent absence of a marrow response in every treated patient','Effective replacement generally restores erythropoiesis.'),('Immediate guaranteed resolution of all neurological deficits within hours','Neurological recovery is slower and may be incomplete.'),('No need to reassess symptoms if the serum B12 becomes high','A post-treatment concentration does not capture the full clinical response.')],
 'Response has a time course and more than one endpoint', '**Marrow recovery precedes full Hb normalization.**',['A reticulocyte rise is an early response clue.','Coexisting iron deficiency can become apparent as erythropoiesis accelerates.'], '**Review the patient rather than only the replacement level.**',['Persistent or worsening symptoms need reassessment.','The cause determines whether replacement continues lifelong.'],[['Early','Reticulocyte response'],['Later','CBC recovery'],['Neurology','May recover slowly or incompletely']],[B12,FO])
